+Each request renews the session for 30 minutes; the [expiry calculation](../../larder/sessions.py) defines the limit.
+Until March 2024 the limit was 10 minutes: members packing a pantry order on a slow mobile connection were logged
+out before they confirmed it
 and lost what they had picked, and support logged 120 tickets about it that month. The limit was raised to 30
 minutes, the shortest that ended those tickets.
 
-## Storage
+## Storage and deployment
 
-Sessions live in Postgres ([keep sessions in the database](../adr/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md)).
+The browser holds the member identity and expiry in a signed cookie. Every web server verifies it with the same
+key, so requests can reach any server. The former Postgres sessions table has been dropped.
+
+Deploy secrets supply `LARDER_SESSION_KEY`. All web servers must use the same key and preserve it across deploys
+to keep existing cookies valid. The [implementation](../../larder/sessions.py) reads the key at module import and
+falls back to `dev-only` when it is absent; deployments must supply the secret rather than rely on that fallback.
+It also defines the cookie encoding, signing and verification.
+
+## Known limits
+
+- The server cannot revoke one member's session before it expires. Without renewing requests, the
+  [session lifetime](#lifetime) bounds how long it remains valid; immediate individual revocation is unsupported.
+- Changing the shared key invalidates every existing session, so members must log in again. Schedule rotation
+  [between pickups](../adr/0001-keep-sessions-in-signed-cookies.md#consequences).
 
